      "content": "Take a deep breath, close your eyes for a second, and let's exhale. You are prepared for this, and we can figure this out together! 🌟\n\nWhen a wave hits an obstacle or a sharp edge, it doesn't just stop; it bends and spreads out into the shadowed region behind that edge. That specific wave phenomenon is called **diffraction**. \n\nNow, look back at the four options provided. Which letter corresponds to diffraction?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, breathing... thank you. So spreading around a sharp edge is diffraction, which is option C right?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Learner correctly identified option C. Provide final answer and required formula/concept breakdown and exam tip according to guidelines.",
      "content": "Yes, it is option C! 🎉\n\n**Final Answer:** C\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Concept:** Wave Diffraction (the bending and spreading of wave fronts around corners or through narrow openings).\n* **What the terms mean:**\n  * **Diffraction:** The spreading of waves when they encounter an obstacle or aperture comparable in size to their wavelength.\n  * **Snell's Law:** Relates the angles of incidence and refraction when light travels between two different media ($n_1\\sin\\theta_1 = n_2\\sin\\theta_2$).\n  * **Total Internal Reflection:** When light reflects entirely back into a denser medium instead of refracting out.\n  * **Huygens' Principle:** A geometric construction stating that every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets.\n* **Why we use it:** Knowing standard definitions word-for-word is crucial for scoring quick marks in Section A multiple-choice questions in your CAPS exams.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Be careful not to mix up *Diffraction* (the phenomenon of spreading) with *Huygens' Principle* (the wave model used to explain why waves propagate and diffract)! Examiners love testing this distinction."
    }
