      "content": "A straight line passing through the origin represents a direct proportionality between $F$ and $E$.\n\n**Final Answer:** B\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $F = qE$ (derived from the definition of electric field $E = \\frac{F}{q}$)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $F$ is the electrostatic force experienced by the charge (measured in Newtons, $\\text{N}$)\n  * $q$ is the magnitude of the test charge (measured in Coulombs, $\\text{C}$)\n  * $E$ is the electric field at that point (measured in Newtons per Coulomb, $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{C}^{-1}$ or $\\text{V}\\cdot\\text{m}^{-1}$)\n* **Why we use it:** Since the charge $q$ is a constant scaling factor, force ($F$) scales linearly with electric field ($E$). When you plot $F$ on the vertical axis and $E$ on the horizontal axis, the slope of the line equals the charge $q$.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In multiple-choice questions involving graphs, always write down the algebraic equation first ($y = mx + c$) and match your variables to the axes to instantly see if the graph should be linear, parabolic, or inversely proportional!"
